From 4659471fd507bae9c0814387b2d61b145a39825d Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Claude <claude@anthropic.com>
Date: Thu, 23 Jul 2026 21:42:17 +0000
Subject: [PATCH] Fix real code-hash bug behind twin-suite flakiness; commit
 the twin live-infra setup

Root cause (not test flakiness -- a real bug in the production cassette
loading path): CassetteLoader.load_cassette() dynamically exec'd cassette
modules via spec_from_file_location/exec_module without ever registering
them in sys.modules. inspect.getmodule() on any object it produced
therefore returned None, and cassette_forensics.compute_cassette_code_hash
silently fell back to an 'UNAVAILABLE_CASSETTE_SOURCE' marker instead of
the cassette's real source. The SAME cassette class loaded two different
ways (CassetteLoader, the actual production path, vs a direct import, used
by some tests) then hashed DIFFERENTLY -- and whichever hash got bound to
a cassette_version first in the ledger would permanently conflict with the
other for the life of that ledger, tripping bind_cassette_version's
tamper-detection tripwire on a false positive. This is what was surfacing
as a cascade of unrelated-looking failures (ValueError: Cassette version
binding conflict, followed by 'permission denied for table ledger_entries'
as later tests failed mid-fixture as a side effect) whenever the full
suite ran more than once against the same persistent ledger. It also meant
the code-hash tamper-evidence mechanism was silently non-functional for
the real production cassette-loading path, independent of testing.

Fix (cassette_loader.py): load from the real cassettes/ package via a
normal importlib.import_module(cassettes.X) when that's what
cassette_dir resolves to -- byte-identical to every other import in the
codebase, so the resulting code hash always matches a direct import of the
same class. Falls back to the low-level dynamic-loading recipe only for a
genuinely external cassette_dir, and that fallback now correctly registers
the module in sys.modules (set before exec_module, matching CPython's own
import machinery) so inspect.getmodule/getsource resolve it at all.

Also, while root-causing this by chasing every layer of the failure by
hand:

- test_twin_live.py: fixed a hardcoded REPO path
  ('/home/claude/sentinel_os/sentinel_os', a leftover from whichever
  dev container originally built this suite) to compute itself from the
  file's own location -- broke immediately on any other machine. Fixed a
  real FEED_DSN password bug: this file hardcoded ledger_reader's
  password as a literal different from the one conftest.py resets it to
  every session, so the two suites silently disagreed on a shared
  credential whenever both ran in one pytest session. FEED_DSN is now
  built lazily from the same env var conftest.py sets.

- conftest.py: grant sentinelsvc membership in ledger_reader from the
  same fixture that creates ledger_reader, closing an ordering race
  against test_twin_live.py's own session-scoped services fixture (the
  self-heal script only grants membership if ledger_reader already
  exists at the moment it runs, and fixture order between two
  independent session-scoped autouse fixtures isn't guaranteed).

- scripts/twin_ensure_services.sh (new): the actual infrastructure this
  suite needs -- three OS identities (sentinelsvc/twincustomer/
  twincustodian), matching peer-auth Postgres roles, sentinelsvc granted
  into ledger_reader -- captured as real, idempotent, committed code.
  This used to exist only as ad-hoc setup performed once, live, inside
  whichever dev container originally built the twin, which is exactly
  why it broke the instant anyone (Wm's Chromebook, this session) tried
  to run it anywhere else. Install as /usr/local/bin/twin_ensure_services
  (the path test_twin_live.py's services fixture already calls).

- .github/workflows/tests.yml, README.md: test_twin_live.py is still
  excluded from the GitHub Actions workflow specifically, but the reason
  is now accurate: peer auth requires the test process and Postgres to
  share a Unix domain socket, and this workflow's
  block is a separate Docker container reached only over TCP -- there's
  no socket to share. Closing that would mean giving this CI job a
  natively-installed Postgres instead of the services: container, a
  separable follow-up, not a one-line fix. The old comment (infra 'never
  committed to this repo') is stale as of this commit -- it is now.

Verified: full repo suite (no --ignore, all 383 tests: 341 original C2
baseline + 24 C2 input-side-checker tests + 18 twin-live tests) run twice
back-to-back from a clean database with zero manual reset in between --
383/383 both times. Previously this same back-to-back run reliably failed
the second time with the cascade described above; confirmed reproducible
before the fix and confirmed gone after it, not just reset away.
